\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{An ordered trace test for charge conjugation}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the directions of \ref{fdip-00LZ} on any finite-dimensional
complex Hermitian space, an antiunitary with the
\vocabk{charge conjugation sign}{fdip-017H} #{\eta} exists exactly
when every subset #{I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}} satisfies
##{\trace(\varepsilon_I)=\eta^{|I|}\bar{\trace(\varepsilon_I)}.}
Here #{\varepsilon_I} is the product in increasing order and
#{\varepsilon_\varnothing=\bbI_S}.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{\bar S} be the conjugate space, with canonical
conjugate-linear isometry #{s\mapsto\bar s}. For a linear
operator #{T} on #{S}, set #{\bar T\bar s=\bar{Ts}}.
Use #{\eta\bar\varepsilon_j} as directions on #{\bar S}.
A unitary intertwiner from this space to #{S}, composed with
canonical conjugation, is exactly an antiunitary with the
stated sign. The trace of each product on #{\bar S} is
#{\eta^{|I|}\bar{\trace(\varepsilon_I)}}. Linear equivalence
therefore implies the test.}

\p{To reverse the argument, form a group of pairs #{(a,I)}
with #{a=\pm1} and #{I} a subset of the directions. Multiply by
##{(a,I)(b,I')=
(ab(-1)^{d(I,I')},\,I\mathbin{\triangle}I'),}
where #{d(I,I')} counts pairs #{i\in I,j\in I'} with #{i>j} and
#{\triangle} is symmetric difference. Modulo two, #{d} is
additive in each subset under symmetric difference. Expanding
the two exponents in a triple product gives associativity.
The identity is #{(1,\varnothing)}. Each square is a scalar
sign, so multiplication by that same sign times the original
pair gives the inverse. Reordering directions uses precisely
these interchanges and deletes equal pairs by
#{\varepsilon_j^2=\bbI_S}. Thus #{(a,I)\mapsto a\varepsilon_I}
is a unitary representation, and so is the corresponding action
from the twisted directions on #{\bar S}.}

\p{The trace test gives equality of characters on both signs
of every subset, hence on this entire finite group.
By \ref{fdip-017J} a unitary intertwiner exists. Its relations
at the singletons give the required conjugation.}
}
